a, sort of my third career to start to

00:03:03.105 --> 00:03:09.015
focus in on that, I did study online
and of course in bookstores, when

00:03:09.015 --> 00:03:12.195
I, you know, 20 years ago, it wasn't
that common to find stuff online.

00:03:12.405 --> 00:03:17.025
So I just wanna show you, you
know, so I bought a book, a

00:03:17.025 --> 00:03:18.465
short history of linguistics.

00:03:18.465 --> 00:03:22.095
Well, that's not quite language
learning, uh, English as a second

00:03:22.095 --> 00:03:23.605
language in the mainstream.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.100
Teaching learning and identity.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:27.810
Like people just wanna learn languages.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:29.880
Phrases and idioms.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:33.330
You know, I've talked about this,
don't try to master phrases and idioms.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:35.220
Uh, but let's see.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:41.970
Then I even bought a Swedish book
here ... uh, how languages are

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:48.170
learned and on, you know, and, uh, the
practice of English language teaching.

00:03:48.885 --> 00:03:53.475
And there was a lot of stuff there that
was quite complicated, got into, you

00:03:53.475 --> 00:03:58.425
know, different types of learners, kinetic
learners, oral learners, visual learners,

00:03:58.430 --> 00:03:59.925
all of which is not true of course.

00:04:00.555 --> 00:04:06.615
Um, I understood very early on that, the
key sort of again, call it the Gordian

00:04:06.615 --> 00:04:10.425
knot for measuring where we are in a
language is how many words we know.

00:04:11.025 --> 00:04:12.035
Words are key.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.960
with words, you get the rest of
it, you get the grammar, everything

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.329
flows from having words and yet...

00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:21.300
and so there are many books on
learning vocabulary in another

00:04:21.305 --> 00:04:24.090
language, and yet it all boils down...

00:04:24.095 --> 00:04:28.560
here again, vocabulary, description,
acquisition, and pedagogy.

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:29.520
It goes...

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.170
vocabulary in language teaching.

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:32.090
I read through all these.

00:04:32.609 --> 00:04:37.140
And yet it really boils down
to what Krashen says, read and

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:39.419
listen, get the language in you.

00:04:39.659 --> 00:04:42.840
Get the brain used to the
language, acquire words.

00:04:42.869 --> 00:04:47.070
Naturally, if you do all of these things,
at some point, you're gonna wanna speak.

00:04:47.159 --> 00:04:49.440
It doesn't matter when that point is.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:54.120
Of course you can't be intimidated, but
the better your level of comprehension,

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:57.765
the more words you have at your
disposal the greater likelihood

00:04:57.825 --> 00:05:00.044
that you won't be so intimidated.

00:05:00.495 --> 00:05:08.265
So, and I, I, I not only is Stephen
Krashen astute in his analysis and

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:13.395
research, this is based on research into
how we acquire languages, but he has a

00:05:13.395 --> 00:05:20.745
very pithy, elegant and, um, economic,
in terms of use of words, to the point

00:05:20.745 --> 00:05:23.535
way of explaining how we learn languages.

00:05:23.925 --> 00:05:27.345
